{"id":"votes-by-official","title":"Each supervisor’s votes on lobbied matters, with and without the circle’s money","question":"Is there any supervisor who voted with lobbyists’ clients far more often when the client’s circle had given to them?","finding":"Across every final Board roll call on disk where a client’s side can be read, 20 supervisors have at least five votes on each side to compare; 1 voted the client’s way more often when the circle had given, beyond the 5% chance level, where chance alone would produce about 1.0. That one is London N. Breed: 97.3% of 37 votes against 83.7% of 49, but 8 of the 9 votes against a client were cast on roll calls with no dissent, where every member voted against the client, so the gap mostly reflects which clients’ circles had given, not votes that differed from colleagues’. On divided roll calls alone 0 supervisors have five votes on each side, and 0 cross that level. Across all 33 supervisors the votes cast against a lobbying client number 264 of 3,297, 60 of them on divided roll calls. On lobbied land-use matters alone, the test as first run, 12 supervisors could be compared and 0 went beyond chance (about 0.6 expected).","how":"The member votes of the widest row of the previous screen (lobbied land-use matters, other measures a lobbyist cited by file number, and money files naming a lobbying client, each file once), one row per supervisor. The test is a one-sided exact test on that supervisor’s own four counts (with or against the client, circle gave or not), run only when there are five or more votes on each side. The test pools a supervisor’s votes across roll calls, so it can be moved by which roll calls a supervisor took part in: where a client opposed a measure that passed with no dissent, every member voted against that client. It is therefore run a second time on divided roll calls alone, where a vote can differ from a colleague’s. The last column counts the supervisor’s votes in the land-use test, which was the whole of this screen before the wider roll calls were on disk.","limits":"Almost every vote here is an aye on a matter that passed without dissent, so most supervisors show the same share on both sides and the test can find little; on divided roll calls few supervisors have five votes on each side. With 20 supervisors each tested at the 5% level, one or two can cross it by chance alone. A supervisor’s district, committee seat and own politics decide which matters they hear from lobbyists about, and donors give to members who already share their outlook. One supervisor’s votes at the same meeting are not independent of each other. A supervisor who served for a short time has few votes here. 2 files (160358, 160657) are left out wherever a final vote is counted or tested: for their last passage vote the vote page lists no member and the minutes print the action twice with different tallies, so it is not counted as unanimous and no earlier reading stands in for it. Lobbying is registered, and so is giving by a lobbyist’s clients and colleagues. An official can sponsor or vote for something a donor wants because the official already favoured it; a donor can give to an official because of what the official already stands for. A private payment for an official act cannot be seen in any of these records, and nothing here says one was made. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","tier":"CLEARED","columns":[{"k":"official","t":"Supervisor","type":"person"},{"k":"district","t":"District","type":"text"},{"k":"votes","t":"Votes where a client’s side can be read","type":"int"},{"k":"gaveVotes","t":"Where the client’s circle had given","type":"int"},{"k":"withGave","t":"Voted the client’s way","type":"pct"},{"k":"otherVotes","t":"Where it had not","type":"int"},{"k":"withOther","t":"Voted the client’s way","type":"pct"},{"k":"contactedVotes","t":"Where the lobbyist contacted them","type":"int"},{"k":"withContacted","t":"Voted the client’s way","type":"pct"},{"k":"against","t":"Votes against the client","type":"int"},{"k":"againstDivided","t":"Of those, on divided roll calls","type":"int"},{"k":"beyond","t":"More with-the-client when given to, beyond chance?","type":"text"},{"k":"divVotes","t":"Votes on divided roll calls","type":"int"},{"k":"divGaveVotes","t":"Of those, where the circle had given","type":"int"},{"k":"divWithGave","t":"Voted the client’s way","type":"pct"},{"k":"divOtherVotes","t":"Where it had not","type":"int"},{"k":"divWithOther","t":"Voted the client’s way","type":"pct"},{"k":"beyondDivided","t":"Beyond chance on divided roll calls?","type":"text"},{"k":"landUseVotes","t":"Votes in the land-use test alone","type":"int"}],"rows":[{"official":"Ahsha Safai","district":"11","votes":210,"gaveVotes":47,"withGave":0.9787,"otherVotes":163,"withOther":0.9141,"contactedVotes":51,"withContacted":0.8824,"against":15,"againstDivided":2,"beyond":"no","divVotes":13,"divGaveVotes":3,"divWithGave":1.0,"divOtherVotes":10,"divWithOther":0.8,"beyondDivided":"too few to test","landUseVotes":60},{"official":"Rafael Mandelman","district":"8","votes":221,"gaveVotes":38,"withGave":1.0,"otherVotes":183,"withOther":0.9344,"contactedVotes":55,"withContacted":0.8727,"against":12,"againstDivided":1,"beyond":"no","divVotes":17,"divGaveVotes":4,"divWithGave":1.0,"divOtherVotes":13,"divWithOther":0.9231,"beyondDivided":"too few to test","landUseVotes":55},{"official":"London N. Breed","district":"5","votes":86,"gaveVotes":37,"withGave":0.973,"otherVotes":49,"withOther":0.8367,"contactedVotes":15,"withContacted":0.7333,"against":9,"againstDivided":1,"beyond":"yes","divVotes":6,"divGaveVotes":0,"divWithGave":null,"divOtherVotes":6,"divWithOther":0.8333,"beyondDivided":"too few to test","landUseVotes":24},{"official":"Aaron Peskin","district":"3","votes":234,"gaveVotes":36,"withGave":0.9167,"otherVotes":198,"withOther":0.904,"